# agent-session board-driver configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env`. The driver reads it from the current working directory.

REPO=owner/name
REPO_PATH=/absolute/path/to/target/repo
SKILL_DIR=/absolute/path/to/skills/agent-session
BOARD=owner/9

# --- GitHub credentials (issue #191) ----------------------------------------
#
# The driver runs under its OWN GitHub account -- a machine user you created for
# it, not yours. Two tokens on that one account:
#
#   - the agent gets a READ-SCOPED token and cannot write to GitHub at all
#   - the driver gets a WRITE token and performs the run's writes itself
#
# The credential is the containment layer. An agent holding a read-only token
# cannot merge, cannot rewrite an issue body and cannot force-push, whatever it
# decides to run. The prompt wording and the PreToolUse merge-block hook are
# defence in depth behind it, not a substitute for it.
#
# There is NO fallback to the host `gh` keyring. All three variables below are
# required, both tokens are checked against a live `gh api user`, and the driver
# refuses to start if either belongs to somebody other than DRIVER_GH_LOGIN. A
# fallback is reached by omission -- an unexported variable, a cron with a clean
# environment -- and its result is a commit or comment attributed to a human who
# did not make it.

# The account both tokens belong to. Not a secret; it is also what stops the
# driver's own park comments reading as human replies and unparking the issues it
# just parked (issue #183) -- a PAT-backed account has no `[bot]` suffix, so
# nothing about the login itself says machine.
DRIVER_GH_LOGIN=your-agent-account

# AGENT_GH_READ_TOKEN: a fine-grained PAT on that account, read-only on the target
# repos -- Metadata, Contents, Issues, Pull requests, Actions and Checks, all Read.
# Nothing else. Safe to keep here: the agent can read this file, and read is all it
# grants.
AGENT_GH_READ_TOKEN=github_pat_read_only_...

# DRIVER_GH_WRITE_TOKEN: a SECOND fine-grained PAT on the same account, with
# Contents, Issues and Pull requests at Write. Two tokens, not one -- reuse a
# single token for both and the agent holds the write credential, which swaps
# whose account leaks rather than fixing the leak. The driver refuses that.
#
# Keep it here. `.env` is git-ignored, and that is the property that matters: the
# driver refuses a credential in a file git would happily add, because a token in
# a tracked file is one `git add -A` from being published and that does not
# un-happen. It does not care where else the token lives.
#
# Being honest about why: putting it somewhere "safer" buys nothing against the
# agent. The agent runs as you with a shell -- it can read this file, ~/.zshrc, or
# replay a keychain command, since `security` trusts its own binary and not the
# caller. Anything the driver can obtain unattended, the agent can obtain the same
# way. That is accepted, not solved: the split contains every ordinary path,
# because the agent's environment holds only the read token; an agent that goes
# deliberately digging is out of scope. See docs/usage.md for what bounds the
# damage instead (PAT scoping, no Workflows permission, branch protection).
DRIVER_GH_WRITE_TOKEN=github_pat_write_...

# Optional, if a deployment wants the secret out of plaintext: any credential
# variable also accepts a `_CMD` form, whose stdout is used. And the driver reads
# ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/agent-session/credentials.env (mode 0600, override
# with DRIVER_CREDENTIALS_FILE) after this file, so project settings still win.
#
#   DRIVER_GH_WRITE_TOKEN_CMD=security find-generic-password -s agent-session-write -w

# Optional: extra logins to treat as machines when deciding whether a comment is a
# human reply. Comma-separated. `github-actions[bot]` and DRIVER_GH_LOGIN are
# always included.
# DRIVER_BOT_LOGINS=dependabot[bot],renovate[bot]

# --- models and budget ------------------------------------------------------

HIGH_TIER_MODEL=
LOW_TIER_MODEL=
MAX_BUDGET_USD=10.0
MAX_ISSUES=1
